WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.075 --> 00:00:03.620
- Hey YouTubeiverse, coming
right up, a cosmic queries

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:06.263
edition of StarTalk, bioethics.

00:00:06.263 --> 00:00:08.846
(upbeat music)

00:00:10.818 --> 00:00:13.706
Welcome to StarTalk, I'm your
host Neil deGrasse Tyson,

00:00:13.706 --> 00:00:15.610
your personal astrophyscist.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:19.022
We've got a cosmic queries
edition of StarTalk.

00:00:19.022 --> 00:00:20.939
The subject, bioethics.

00:00:25.145 --> 00:00:26.766
My co-host Paul Mecurio.

00:00:26.766 --> 00:00:27.599
Paul!

00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:28.432
- Nice to see you again.

00:00:28.432 --> 00:00:29.265
- Welcome back dude.

00:00:29.265 --> 00:00:30.098
- Yeah, thanks for having me back.

00:00:30.098 --> 00:00:30.950
- Thanks for making some time for us

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.653
before warming up Steven
Colbert's audience.

00:00:34.653 --> 00:00:35.486
- You're right down the street.

00:00:35.486 --> 00:00:37.017
- Yeah, which is up the
street from here yeah.

00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:39.537
- Yeah and you provide
a limo which is nice.

00:00:39.537 --> 00:00:41.433
(laughing)

00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:42.266
- Did we?

00:00:42.266 --> 00:00:43.099
- [Paul] No.

00:00:43.099 --> 00:00:44.484
- Okay.

00:00:44.484 --> 00:00:46.031
Now, you're not a bioethicist.

00:00:46.031 --> 00:00:46.864
- No.

00:00:46.864 --> 00:00:47.728
- Neither am I.

00:00:47.728 --> 00:00:49.012
Even though we might have
thoughts on the matter.

00:00:49.012 --> 00:00:50.021
Right?

00:00:50.021 --> 00:00:51.597
- Every day I'm constantly,

00:00:51.597 --> 00:00:52.763
(laughing)

00:00:52.763 --> 00:00:55.593
I wake up and go what
is going on ethically?

00:00:55.593 --> 00:00:56.931
- Bioethically.

00:00:56.931 --> 00:01:00.124
So we went back into our Rolodex

00:01:00.124 --> 00:01:03.264
and reinvited Professor Matthew Liao.

00:01:03.264 --> 00:01:04.752
Matthew, welcome back to StarTalk.

00:01:04.752 --> 00:01:05.585
- Thank you.

00:01:05.585 --> 00:01:07.207
- So we last had you on stage

00:01:07.207 --> 00:01:09.945
live in front of an audience
at New York Comic-Con.

00:01:09.945 --> 00:01:12.145
And we had Adam Savage with us as well

00:01:12.145 --> 00:01:14.310
and we were talking
about human augmentation

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.393
and whether that would be bioethical.

00:01:18.305 --> 00:01:20.557
And you said off camera, you remember,

00:01:20.557 --> 00:01:23.217
that that was my birthday?

00:01:23.217 --> 00:01:24.181
- We sung, right?

00:01:24.181 --> 00:01:25.274
- [Neil] Oh I try to forget those things.

00:01:25.274 --> 00:01:27.624
- Yes I think 3,000 people sung to you.

00:01:27.624 --> 00:01:30.463
- [Neil] They did, they all
sang, they all did sing.

00:01:30.463 --> 00:01:34.938
- It was written on a program
that that was required.

00:01:34.938 --> 00:01:38.156
- So welcome back and good to have you.

00:01:38.156 --> 00:01:41.656
You are Director of the Bioethics program.

00:01:42.826 --> 00:01:44.736
- The Center for Bioethics at NYU.

00:01:44.736 --> 00:01:46.464
- [Neil] At NYU, Center for Bioethics

00:01:46.464 --> 00:01:48.685
and New York University
right here in town.

00:01:48.685 --> 00:01:49.759
So easy date for you.

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:51.066
So we'll be calling more on you.

00:01:51.066 --> 00:01:51.899
- Oh great.

00:01:51.899 --> 00:01:53.503
- As we think of these issues.

00:01:53.503 --> 00:01:55.040
So we've got questions, Paul.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:55.873
- [Paul] Yes.

00:01:55.873 --> 00:01:58.169
- I haven't seen them, I don't
know if Matthew's seen them.

00:01:58.169 --> 00:02:01.492
- No he has not and we'll
just sort of jump right in.

00:02:01.492 --> 00:02:03.361
Well let me just find
out, what is bioethics?

00:02:03.361 --> 00:02:04.390
What's an example?

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.042
Just, so we're on the same page.

00:02:06.042 --> 00:02:08.887
- Yeah, it's the study of biomedic issues

00:02:08.887 --> 00:02:11.005
arising out of biomedical technologies.

00:02:11.005 --> 00:02:12.069
- [Neil] Mostly medical now.

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:15.359
- Yep, mostly medical
but it could also involve

00:02:15.359 --> 00:02:17.214
things like artificial intelligence

00:02:17.214 --> 00:02:20.847
and sort of it's connection to healthcare.

00:02:20.847 --> 00:02:24.366
- Yeah but AI's not bio on purpose.

00:02:24.366 --> 00:02:26.688
- Right, but it could be.

00:02:26.688 --> 00:02:28.025
- [Neil] So what you
want is silicon ethics.

00:02:28.025 --> 00:02:30.134
- Yeah, silicon ethics, that's right.

00:02:30.134 --> 00:02:31.454
Well a lot of people are now thinking

00:02:31.454 --> 00:02:34.278
about putting in things like
brain computer interfaces

00:02:34.278 --> 00:02:37.254
into their brains and things like that,

00:02:37.254 --> 00:02:39.820
so the silicon and the organic matter,

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.756
they're kind of merging right now.

00:02:41.756 --> 00:02:43.464
- So this complicates your job.

00:02:43.464 --> 00:02:44.297
- [Matthew] That's right.

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:46.586
- Or makes it more interesting, both.

00:02:46.586 --> 00:02:48.058
- What's the fastest moving area?

00:02:48.058 --> 00:02:48.891
Is it AI?

00:02:50.166 --> 00:02:52.416
Is it genetic manipulation?

00:02:53.666 --> 00:02:57.733
- Yeah I think both of them
are occurring concurrently so,

00:02:57.733 --> 00:02:59.155
there's the CRISPR technology,

00:02:59.155 --> 00:03:01.913
genetic technology
that's really advancing.

00:03:01.913 --> 00:03:03.912
- I like that, because if
you can mutate my genes

00:03:03.912 --> 00:03:05.243
so I don't have to go to the gym,

00:03:05.243 --> 00:03:06.852
then I'm a happier guy.

00:03:06.852 --> 00:03:07.796
- Is that how that works?

00:03:07.796 --> 00:03:08.629
(laughing)

00:03:08.629 --> 00:03:10.751
- That's exactly how it
works, you can do it today.

00:03:10.751 --> 00:03:13.160
(group laughing)

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.678
And then there's the
artificial intelligence.

00:03:15.678 --> 00:03:18.222
People are using that
for things like cancer,

00:03:18.222 --> 00:03:22.517
you know, there are pathologists
looking at these images.

00:03:22.517 --> 00:03:25.597
The AI's getting really
good at pattern recognition

00:03:25.597 --> 00:03:26.824
and image recognition.

00:03:26.824 --> 00:03:28.985
They can spot cancer cells

00:03:28.985 --> 00:03:32.552
almost as good as pathologists now.

00:03:32.552 --> 00:03:35.061
- So but that wouldn't be an ethical thing

00:03:35.061 --> 00:03:36.418
that's just, the machine can do it better

00:03:36.418 --> 00:03:37.566
so let the machine do it.

00:03:37.566 --> 00:03:38.399
- Right.

00:03:38.399 --> 00:03:39.232
- Right, so ethics would be

00:03:39.232 --> 00:03:40.945
now the machine knows your condition

00:03:40.945 --> 00:03:43.030
and it's connected to the internet,

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.241
and so a hacker might have access.

00:03:45.241 --> 00:03:49.408
- Yeah, or say that you
know the insurance company

00:03:52.464 --> 00:03:55.355
knows the algorithm and tries to hack it

00:03:55.355 --> 00:03:58.268
and so to make it look like
it's not cancer when it is,

00:03:58.268 --> 00:03:59.461
or something like that.

00:03:59.461 --> 00:04:02.004
Or issues to do with privacy.

00:04:02.004 --> 00:04:04.807
- Wow he's paid to think about this stuff.

00:04:04.807 --> 00:04:06.292
- You have a very diabolical mind.

00:04:06.292 --> 00:04:07.733
(group laughing)

00:04:07.733 --> 00:04:10.399
- You know, come up with a way
that we can foil this system.

00:04:10.399 --> 00:04:11.300
- When you're out for dinner,

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:13.445
and the waitress said, would
you like to have dessert,

00:04:13.445 --> 00:04:14.608
you're like, what do you mean by that?

00:04:14.608 --> 00:04:16.862
(laughing)

00:04:16.862 --> 00:04:18.153
Are you fun around people?

00:04:18.153 --> 00:04:20.516
I mean you're fun, but like
if they wanted to do something

00:04:20.516 --> 00:04:21.982
a little inappropriate,

00:04:21.982 --> 00:04:24.695
like put a little extra
gas in when nobody notices.

00:04:24.695 --> 00:04:27.138
You go like, no there's
an ethical issue there?

00:04:27.138 --> 00:04:29.526
- [Neil] How ethical are
you, is the question there.

00:04:29.526 --> 00:04:32.702
- Well there are surveys that say that

00:04:32.702 --> 00:04:36.642
ethicists aren't necessarily more ethical.

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:40.475
So apparently they steal
books from libraries,

00:04:42.378 --> 00:04:46.219
and they don't call their
mothers on Mother's Day,

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:47.782
and things like that.

00:04:47.782 --> 00:04:48.798
I call my mom on Mother's Day.

00:04:48.798 --> 00:04:51.398
- Okay, so it's do as you
say and not as you do?

00:04:51.398 --> 00:04:53.450
- That's right.

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.412
- [Neil] All right, so what
questions you have Paul?

00:04:55.412 --> 00:04:57.455
- We're gonna start with
the Patreon question.

00:04:57.455 --> 00:04:59.167
- [Neil] Patreon, let's do it.

00:04:59.167 --> 00:05:00.192
Give some to the Patreon.

00:05:00.192 --> 00:05:01.451
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:05:01.451 --> 00:05:02.284
We love them.

00:05:02.284 --> 00:05:04.300
This is Oliver Gigas.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.077
I'm sorry if I'm mispronouncing that.

00:05:07.077 --> 00:05:09.475
"Personally I feel that
we the general public

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:13.785
aren't talking enough about
subjects like bioethics and AI.

00:05:13.785 --> 00:05:14.977
Even though they are clearly going

00:05:14.977 --> 00:05:16.464
to be a huge part of the future.

00:05:16.464 --> 00:05:19.201
Do either of you feel
the same way and if so,

00:05:19.201 --> 00:05:22.853
how can we better educate
ourselves on these subjects?"

00:05:22.853 --> 00:05:24.372
- I completely agree.

00:05:24.372 --> 00:05:27.491
And so one of the things I try to do

00:05:27.491 --> 00:05:31.741
is to talk to the public
about some of these issues

00:05:33.339 --> 00:05:37.672
and the work in this area,
things like gene editing,

00:05:38.629 --> 00:05:40.535
and artificial intelligence.

00:05:40.535 --> 00:05:42.950
- How much of it is just fear

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:45.145
that people don't
understand the technology?

00:05:45.145 --> 00:05:48.573
And so we fear everything
we don't understand.

00:05:48.573 --> 00:05:50.275
Doesn't it come down
to that at some level?

00:05:50.275 --> 00:05:52.093
- Yeah, I think a lot of it is that.

00:05:52.093 --> 00:05:55.113
Just people are scared
of new technologies,

00:05:55.113 --> 00:05:57.427
they're very cautious.

00:05:57.427 --> 00:05:58.829
- You have great science fiction writers

00:05:58.829 --> 00:06:02.501
that take it to the worst dystopic future.

00:06:02.501 --> 00:06:05.021
- That's right, you know
the robots are after us,

00:06:05.021 --> 00:06:08.441
they're gonna kill us,
superintelligence is coming,

00:06:08.441 --> 00:06:10.254
and so people get really scared

00:06:10.254 --> 00:06:13.531
and they think oh we should
not do any of this stuff,

00:06:13.531 --> 00:06:16.499
and that's also bad for
science, it's bad for progress.

00:06:16.499 --> 00:06:17.426
- Yeah but I just bought a car

00:06:17.426 --> 00:06:19.187
where I don't have a dipstick anymore

00:06:19.187 --> 00:06:21.379
and I just hit a button
and it tells me the oil,

00:06:21.379 --> 00:06:22.212
the oil stats.

00:06:22.212 --> 00:06:23.045
- [Neil] Really?

00:06:23.045 --> 00:06:23.878
- Yeah.

00:06:23.878 --> 00:06:24.841
And I'm a little weirded out by that.

00:06:24.841 --> 00:06:26.466
Like I want the physical thing.

00:06:26.466 --> 00:06:28.412
- Get off my lawn!

00:06:28.412 --> 00:06:30.126
I'm not an old man yet!

00:06:30.126 --> 00:06:31.003
- But I don't trust them.

00:06:31.003 --> 00:06:32.134
- Young whippersnapper!

00:06:32.134 --> 00:06:34.687
- What if the oil companies have

00:06:34.687 --> 00:06:37.179
adjusted the program of that

00:06:37.179 --> 00:06:39.574
so that it's falsely
telling me I need oil,

00:06:39.574 --> 00:06:40.471
to make extra money?

00:06:40.471 --> 00:06:41.304
- Yeah.

00:06:41.304 --> 00:06:42.835
- We should hang out,
you see what I'm saying?

00:06:42.835 --> 00:06:45.066
- Yeah, you sound like
a bioethicist already.

00:06:45.066 --> 00:06:47.316
(laughing)

00:06:48.237 --> 00:06:50.440
- Not to hang you out to dry.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:53.523
When the dashboard became all screen,

00:06:54.981 --> 00:06:57.096
without a mechanical speedometer,

00:06:57.096 --> 00:06:59.418
where it just, it turns on.

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:02.705
And in what turns on, it has your mileage.

00:07:02.705 --> 00:07:06.705
And I'm thinking, this
is a screen, come on now.

00:07:07.756 --> 00:07:11.431
There's no mechanical miles (mumbling),

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:13.788
and I got all Old Man on it.

00:07:13.788 --> 00:07:15.530
I said, give me back my dial!

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:16.642
(laughing)

00:07:16.642 --> 00:07:18.300
- I'm with you, I unplug
my toaster everything night

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.374
because I think it's gonna catch fire.

00:07:19.374 --> 00:07:20.746
I don't know.

00:07:20.746 --> 00:07:21.932
The whole thing is sort of

00:07:21.932 --> 00:07:23.828
overwhelming for people on some level.

00:07:23.828 --> 00:07:26.406
- Yeah, so I think you hit
on exactly the right issue.

00:07:26.406 --> 00:07:28.974
And the issue is trust.

00:07:28.974 --> 00:07:32.079
Like trust in technology,
trust in algorithms,

00:07:32.079 --> 00:07:34.240
trust in how do we make that

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.868
when we roll out these
technologies there's trust?

00:07:36.868 --> 00:07:40.134
And that's the job of the scientists

00:07:40.134 --> 00:07:41.731
but also the ethicists and--

00:07:41.731 --> 00:07:42.564
- [Neil] And the educator.

00:07:42.564 --> 00:07:43.614
- Yeah, and the educator.

00:07:43.614 --> 00:07:45.920
To make sure that we can
actually trust these things.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:47.577
- So here's a question that I remembered

00:07:47.577 --> 00:07:49.632
getting asked of the public.

00:07:49.632 --> 00:07:51.289
And I remembered at the time

00:07:51.289 --> 00:07:53.937
what my answer was then
and it still is today.

00:07:53.937 --> 00:07:56.387
But the public in the
day answered differently.

00:07:56.387 --> 00:07:58.398
Here is the question.

00:07:58.398 --> 00:07:59.231
If

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:02.463
something happens,

00:08:02.463 --> 00:08:04.112
you're on an airplane,

00:08:04.112 --> 00:08:07.695
and something goes
wrong with the airplane.

00:08:10.992 --> 00:08:12.291
What would you trust?

00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:15.624
A button that says "Auto
fly this thing home",

00:08:15.624 --> 00:08:17.624
or a trained navy pilot,

00:08:18.559 --> 00:08:20.826
a decorated navy pilot to bring it home?

00:08:20.826 --> 00:08:21.996
And everybody says, the pilot of course!

00:08:21.996 --> 00:08:24.695
And I'm thinking, no
give me the autopilot.

00:08:24.695 --> 00:08:25.900
Push the auto button.

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:27.160
- What if he just had
a fight with his wife

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:28.810
and just downed a bottle
of scotch at the airport?

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:31.277
- That's what I'm saying.

00:08:31.277 --> 00:08:34.950
The button didn't have a
bottle of scotch, guaranteed.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.579
And today, I mean what
my thinking has born out,

00:08:38.579 --> 00:08:40.519
because planes are designed

00:08:40.519 --> 00:08:43.629
that they cannot actually
be flown by a human being,

00:08:43.629 --> 00:08:44.781
there's too many surfaces

00:08:44.781 --> 00:08:46.870
that are under control of the computer,

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:49.222
that's why flying is so stable now.

00:08:49.222 --> 00:08:51.910
So do you trust the technology or not?

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:53.549
- [Matthew] That's right, yeah and so

00:08:53.549 --> 00:08:55.057
in order to trust the technology,

00:08:55.057 --> 00:08:56.510
you have to make sure that it's safe,

00:08:56.510 --> 00:08:58.469
it's tested, it's reliable,

00:08:58.469 --> 00:09:01.386
it can't be adversarially attacked,

00:09:03.613 --> 00:09:07.076
and that's why ethicists like myself

00:09:07.076 --> 00:09:08.623
we ask these questions,

00:09:08.623 --> 00:09:10.358
things like, well what happens?

00:09:10.358 --> 00:09:13.071
You know, we imagine these
hypothetical examples,

00:09:13.071 --> 00:09:14.960
like what happens if
the insurance companies

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:17.039
try to cheat you and that sort of thing,

00:09:17.039 --> 00:09:20.160
or if the hackers try to
hack into the algorithm,

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:21.910
or the imaging thing,

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:25.537
there's plenty of evidence that

00:09:25.537 --> 00:09:28.780
some of these machine learning
technologies can be hacked.

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:30.788
- Well the thing that's
amazing to me is science,

00:09:30.788 --> 00:09:35.371
and especially what you do,
is so on track with ethics.

00:09:36.484 --> 00:09:38.965
It's a microcosm because
in society in general,

00:09:38.965 --> 00:09:41.202
ethics seems to be the last thing.

00:09:41.202 --> 00:09:42.793
It's like worrying about table manners

00:09:42.793 --> 00:09:45.492
at a Game of Throne Red Wedding, right?

00:09:45.492 --> 00:09:47.534
You guys have this ability

00:09:47.534 --> 00:09:48.873
to really think about these things.

00:09:48.873 --> 00:09:50.544
Like there's this conversation about

00:09:50.544 --> 00:09:52.837
well AI can destroy the planet.

00:09:52.837 --> 00:09:55.408
Well humans are already
kind of doing that.

00:09:55.408 --> 00:09:56.695
Is that any worse?

00:09:56.695 --> 00:09:58.069
- Yeah, maybe AI can do it better.

00:09:58.069 --> 00:09:59.682
(group laughing)

00:09:59.682 --> 00:10:00.902
- More efficiently.

00:10:00.902 --> 00:10:03.781
- [Neil] Exactly, none
of this dallying about.

00:10:03.781 --> 00:10:05.477
- Less complaining.

00:10:05.477 --> 00:10:09.500
- Well some people think
that superintelligence,

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:10.882
if they were to be created,

00:10:10.882 --> 00:10:15.279
they're gonna decide that, "hey
we're destroying the planet,

00:10:15.279 --> 00:10:18.260
and one way to stop, to help the planet,

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:20.983
is by killing all of us."

00:10:20.983 --> 00:10:23.161
- Because we're a virus.

00:10:23.161 --> 00:10:25.646
- [Matthew] They do it
because we're viruses, yeah.

00:10:25.646 --> 00:10:28.126
- That's the word my wife uses for me.

00:10:28.126 --> 00:10:30.836
- That's a line from
the Matrix, the first.

00:10:30.836 --> 00:10:32.435
All right so Paul, you've
got more questions?

00:10:32.435 --> 00:10:34.001
- [Paul] I do.

00:10:34.001 --> 00:10:36.751
Raymond Oyung, StarTalkRadio.net,

00:10:37.968 --> 00:10:40.027
question about morals and science.

00:10:40.027 --> 00:10:41.885
Are there any circumstances in science

00:10:41.885 --> 00:10:43.037
where it would be acceptable

00:10:43.037 --> 00:10:45.803
to bypass ethics in human experimentation

00:10:45.803 --> 00:10:49.011
if the findings would
lead to greater good?

00:10:49.011 --> 00:10:50.261
- Ooh good one.

00:10:51.274 --> 00:10:55.454
Wasn't that the entire
Nazi medical enterprise?

00:10:55.454 --> 00:10:58.195
And the Tuskegee study.

00:10:58.195 --> 00:10:59.802
- That's the Tuskegee study as well.

00:10:59.802 --> 00:11:03.392
- Yeah just tell us about
one or both of those.

00:11:03.392 --> 00:11:04.613
That's a great question here.

00:11:04.613 --> 00:11:08.696
- Yeah so the Nazis were
experimenting on humans.

00:11:11.155 --> 00:11:13.701
For example, they're taking
them up into the airplanes

00:11:13.701 --> 00:11:18.118
to see how much pressure a
human being can withstand.

00:11:19.101 --> 00:11:21.853
- These were mostly Jews
and other undesirables

00:11:21.853 --> 00:11:24.686
in the Germanic model of humanity?

00:11:26.005 --> 00:11:28.138
- [Matthew] That's right.

00:11:28.138 --> 00:11:31.759
And they apparently some
people say that they were able

00:11:31.759 --> 00:11:35.846
to find out things that we
wouldn't have otherwise found

00:11:35.846 --> 00:11:38.797
but still, I think that
it's very clear now

00:11:38.797 --> 00:11:42.312
that we need to abide
by these ethical norms

00:11:42.312 --> 00:11:45.419
and we need to stick to research ethics.

00:11:45.419 --> 00:11:49.122
And there's something
called the Belmont Report

00:11:49.122 --> 00:11:53.235
that came out as a result
of the Tuskegee experiments.

00:11:53.235 --> 00:11:54.666
- Just describe Tuskegee.

00:11:54.666 --> 00:11:55.666
- Yeah, it's

00:11:57.497 --> 00:11:59.473
the experiment where there were

00:11:59.473 --> 00:12:02.172
these subjects and they
were given syphilis

00:12:02.172 --> 00:12:05.147
and they weren't told that.

00:12:05.147 --> 00:12:06.277
- But I thought they already had syphilis.

00:12:06.277 --> 00:12:07.198
- They already had syphilis.

00:12:07.198 --> 00:12:08.355
- But they were told
they were being treated,

00:12:08.355 --> 00:12:09.585
but in fact they weren't.

00:12:09.585 --> 00:12:11.243
And then the observation was to see

00:12:11.243 --> 00:12:14.493
the progress of syphilis
in the human body.

00:12:14.493 --> 00:12:16.462
And all of the subjects were black men.

00:12:16.462 --> 00:12:18.245
- [Matthew] That's right.

00:12:18.245 --> 00:12:21.772
After that, when it was
discovered, basically,

00:12:21.772 --> 00:12:25.355
that was the birth of
bioethics as a field.

00:12:27.711 --> 00:12:31.628
People decided that we
shouldn't be doing this.

00:12:32.697 --> 00:12:35.927
There are different principles
that were being proposed,

00:12:35.927 --> 00:12:38.468
things like, "do no harm",

00:12:38.468 --> 00:12:42.648
you need to make sure the
research benefits the subject,

00:12:42.648 --> 00:12:44.730
and then you need to make
sure that there's autonomy,

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:46.110
there's informed consent,

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:48.772
so a lot of the bioethical
principles came out as a result.

00:12:48.772 --> 00:12:50.264
(talking over each other)

00:12:50.264 --> 00:12:51.578
- Well yeah "do no harm", that's in there.

00:12:51.578 --> 00:12:53.000
- That's part of the Hippocratic Oath.

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:54.718
Yeah but, talk to Mickey
Rourke's surgeons,

00:12:54.718 --> 00:12:58.387
I mean, they violated that
thing eight ways to Sunday.

00:12:58.387 --> 00:13:00.178
I mean isn't that sort of part of the--

00:13:00.178 --> 00:13:03.168
The medical field to me seems like,

00:13:03.168 --> 00:13:08.017
was it fair to say the
first area where bioethics

00:13:08.017 --> 00:13:09.858
was sort of really founded in some way?

00:13:09.858 --> 00:13:10.691
- Yeah.

00:13:10.691 --> 00:13:11.656
- And yet it seems like that profession,

00:13:11.656 --> 00:13:12.489
they're all over the place.

00:13:12.489 --> 00:13:15.480
I mean there's pimple popper shows on TLC.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:18.100
- Well I think maybe they're
intent is to not do harm,

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:20.264
even if they end up doing harm.

00:13:20.264 --> 00:13:21.103
Right?

00:13:21.103 --> 00:13:22.103
Like plastic surgery.

00:13:22.103 --> 00:13:24.695
It can go wrong, but
it wasn't their intent.

00:13:24.695 --> 00:13:26.047
- It's like me with a bad joke.

00:13:26.047 --> 00:13:27.458
(group laughing)

00:13:27.458 --> 00:13:28.291
- You did harm.

00:13:28.291 --> 00:13:29.507
- A lot of harm.

00:13:29.507 --> 00:13:31.595
That set did a lot of harm,

00:13:31.595 --> 00:13:32.627
and I can't bring it back.

00:13:32.627 --> 00:13:34.065
- [Neil] Okay, so what you're saying is,

00:13:34.065 --> 00:13:39.065
this is an interesting,
enlightened posture which is,

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:43.547
no matter what is going on,
I will do no harm to you,

00:13:43.547 --> 00:13:45.225
even if having done harm to you,

00:13:45.225 --> 00:13:47.622
saves the lives of a hundred other people.

00:13:47.622 --> 00:13:52.557
Because the individual, has
the priority in this exchange,

00:13:52.557 --> 00:13:53.729
in this relationship.

00:13:53.729 --> 00:13:58.224
So that's enlightened and
even profound, I think.

00:13:58.224 --> 00:14:02.268
- Is the converse of this,
what Neil just mentioned,

00:14:02.268 --> 00:14:04.683
this whole issue with measles now,

00:14:04.683 --> 00:14:05.975
because I'm really fascinated by that.

00:14:05.975 --> 00:14:08.944
So someone is morally
against a vaccination

00:14:08.944 --> 00:14:10.416
because they think it causes autism,

00:14:10.416 --> 00:14:14.505
and yet they're putting
entire communities at risk,

00:14:14.505 --> 00:14:18.970
what is the conversation in
your field now about that?

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:20.297
And what do you serve at a measles party?

00:14:20.297 --> 00:14:21.212
Salmonella cake?

00:14:21.212 --> 00:14:22.629
I'm just curious.

00:14:27.654 --> 00:14:29.555
- So my own view about vaccination is

00:14:29.555 --> 00:14:33.234
that we have a public
duty to be vaccinated,

00:14:33.234 --> 00:14:36.910
and so that comes from
not harming other people,

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:39.629
so we have an obligation
not to harm other people.

00:14:39.629 --> 00:14:40.462
And so,

00:14:43.215 --> 00:14:46.132
the issue with vaccination is that,

00:14:47.143 --> 00:14:48.575
it's the bodily integrity.

00:14:48.575 --> 00:14:50.456
We also have the right
to bodily integrity,

00:14:50.456 --> 00:14:53.248
so some people think that
we shouldn't be forced

00:14:53.248 --> 00:14:55.834
to be vaccinated if we don't want to.

00:14:55.834 --> 00:14:57.605
And I think that's right,

00:14:57.605 --> 00:14:59.320
but I also think that,

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:00.661
that doesn't mean that we ourselves

00:15:00.661 --> 00:15:02.254
don't have a duty to be vaccinated,

00:15:02.254 --> 00:15:05.589
so we should do it voluntarily.

00:15:05.589 --> 00:15:06.556
- So there's a greater good.

00:15:06.556 --> 00:15:07.389
- That's right.

00:15:07.389 --> 00:15:08.252
It's a greater good argument.

00:15:08.252 --> 00:15:10.694
- That overrides the personal integrity.

00:15:10.694 --> 00:15:13.785
- Well personal integrity
is something you can wave,

00:15:13.785 --> 00:15:16.073
it's your right, but
you can wave it, right?

00:15:16.073 --> 00:15:17.422
In these cases.

00:15:17.422 --> 00:15:20.494
So in this case I think
that we have a duty to

00:15:20.494 --> 00:15:24.344
serve the public by getting vaccinated.

00:15:24.344 --> 00:15:26.718
- You kinda straddled
the fence there a little.

00:15:26.718 --> 00:15:28.026
Yeah, you don't wanna create a law.

00:15:28.026 --> 00:15:28.859
- You should run for president man.

00:15:28.859 --> 00:15:30.482
- [Neil] (laughs) That was good.

00:15:30.482 --> 00:15:31.891
- You did not answer that question.

00:15:31.891 --> 00:15:32.724
You know what?

00:15:32.724 --> 00:15:35.141
That was an unethical answer.

00:15:36.383 --> 00:15:38.718
- It's interesting,
it's really complicated.

00:15:38.718 --> 00:15:40.599
- And they actually dealt
with a little bit of this

00:15:40.599 --> 00:15:42.121
in "Planet of the Apes."

00:15:42.121 --> 00:15:44.564
Because you have the intelligent chimps,

00:15:44.564 --> 00:15:45.747
and they're doing medical experiments on

00:15:45.747 --> 00:15:47.707
the humans that they captured,

00:15:47.707 --> 00:15:49.716
and we think that's an abomination

00:15:49.716 --> 00:15:52.005
because we're human,
but of course we do that

00:15:52.005 --> 00:15:53.840
on lab animals all the time!

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:55.570
So who are we to say
that they can't do that?

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:57.378
- And yet, the quality of
our life is much better

00:15:57.378 --> 00:16:01.434
because we do it, so it's sort
of this whole balancing act.

00:16:01.434 --> 00:16:02.301
- That's why we have you.

00:16:02.301 --> 00:16:03.134
- Yeah.

00:16:03.134 --> 00:16:04.496
- Yeah, okay (laughs).

00:16:04.496 --> 00:16:07.117
- Not to do experiments on you right?

00:16:07.117 --> 00:16:07.950
That's next week.

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:10.897
You come back and there's a
dungeon and we take you there.

00:16:10.897 --> 00:16:13.230
- Wait, I can't let this go.

00:16:14.974 --> 00:16:17.661
So there's not even
some numerical threshold

00:16:17.661 --> 00:16:18.971
where you would say,

00:16:18.971 --> 00:16:23.893
harm to one person if it saves
a hundred, or a thousand,

00:16:23.893 --> 00:16:26.143
or a million, or a billion?

00:16:27.104 --> 00:16:29.882
- [Matthew] Yeah, so there's
this view it's called

00:16:29.882 --> 00:16:31.632
threshold deontology.

00:16:32.765 --> 00:16:34.163
Threshold deontology.

00:16:34.163 --> 00:16:35.013
- Deontology.

00:16:35.013 --> 00:16:35.852
- That's right.

00:16:35.852 --> 00:16:38.183
And it's the view that,
there's a threshold,

00:16:38.183 --> 00:16:40.437
and when you cross that threshold then

00:16:40.437 --> 00:16:42.591
it might be okay to
harm somebody in order--

00:16:42.591 --> 00:16:43.511
- But isn't it arbitrary?

00:16:43.511 --> 00:16:45.971
Who decides what the threshold is?

00:16:45.971 --> 00:16:46.865
- [Neil] That's why we have him.

00:16:46.865 --> 00:16:48.222
(group laughing)

00:16:48.222 --> 00:16:49.182
- You're making all of these decisions?

00:16:49.182 --> 00:16:50.015
- [Neil] He's the ethicist.

00:16:50.015 --> 00:16:51.355
- You?

00:16:51.355 --> 00:16:53.286
I'm leaving (laughs).

00:16:53.286 --> 00:16:54.414
- You're sitting next to an ethicist.

00:16:54.414 --> 00:16:55.353
Who makes these decisions?

00:16:55.353 --> 00:16:56.394
He makes the decisions.

00:16:56.394 --> 00:16:57.691
He and his people.

00:16:57.691 --> 00:16:58.524
- He and his people.

00:16:58.524 --> 00:16:59.357
He has a team.

00:16:59.357 --> 00:17:02.383
- Yeah, so you're absolutely right.

00:17:02.383 --> 00:17:03.508
So where is the threshold?

00:17:03.508 --> 00:17:07.071
It's not okay to say, kill
one to save five people.

00:17:07.071 --> 00:17:10.193
Like is it okay to kill one
to save a million people?

00:17:10.193 --> 00:17:11.059
- [Neil] Right.

00:17:11.059 --> 00:17:11.892
What is the threshold?

00:17:11.892 --> 00:17:12.725
- Or a billion people.

00:17:12.725 --> 00:17:13.558
What's the threshold?

00:17:13.558 --> 00:17:15.471
- But if one to five is okay,

00:17:15.471 --> 00:17:16.739
- Is not okay.

00:17:16.739 --> 00:17:20.248
- Okay, but then you're
saying, no joke here,

00:17:20.248 --> 00:17:21.840
Neil is one of the five,

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:25.715
but then there's a million
and you're saying it's okay,

00:17:25.715 --> 00:17:27.987
you've devalued his life based on

00:17:27.987 --> 00:17:30.452
the number of people in the group.

00:17:30.452 --> 00:17:32.409
That doesn't seem to be any logic to that.

00:17:32.409 --> 00:17:35.076
- Yeah, so some people say that,

00:17:35.937 --> 00:17:39.063
well if we were to think
that it's okay to kill Neil

00:17:39.063 --> 00:17:41.020
in order to save a billion people--

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:42.335
- [Neil] How did I get
in the middle of this?

00:17:42.335 --> 00:17:44.228
(group laughing)

00:17:44.228 --> 00:17:47.169
- [Paul] Well you're very
smart, extremely intelligent,

00:17:47.169 --> 00:17:49.410
so you're worth a billion people.

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:50.918
I'm worth like a dog.

00:17:50.918 --> 00:17:53.251
I'm the equivalent of a dog.

00:17:54.181 --> 00:17:57.222
- It's the rowboat thing,
you throw out Abe Lincoln,

00:17:57.222 --> 00:17:59.021
do you keep the criminal,
like what do you do?

00:17:59.021 --> 00:18:00.843
- And by the way, how would we kill Neil?

00:18:00.843 --> 00:18:02.281
Just out of curiosity.

00:18:02.281 --> 00:18:04.488
Would it be a slow death?

00:18:04.488 --> 00:18:05.321
- I'm an ethicist not a--

00:18:05.321 --> 00:18:06.430
- The most ethical way to kill me.

00:18:06.430 --> 00:18:08.238
- Watching Lifetime
channel the whole time?

00:18:08.238 --> 00:18:11.321
- Painless, painless way of doing it.

00:18:12.254 --> 00:18:13.865
- [Neil] So tell me it's called the.

00:18:13.865 --> 00:18:15.255
- Threshold deontology.

00:18:15.255 --> 00:18:16.565
- [Neil] Threshold deontology.

00:18:16.565 --> 00:18:19.916
- Yeah and so that's the
view that there's a threshold

00:18:19.916 --> 00:18:22.311
beyond which it's okay to harm somebody

00:18:22.311 --> 00:18:24.889
in order to save a greater number.

00:18:24.889 --> 00:18:27.406
- So, towards the end of the movie

00:18:27.406 --> 00:18:30.672
"The Secret of Santa Vittoria",

00:18:30.672 --> 00:18:32.270
I don't know if it's fiction,

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:34.241
or if it's based on a real story.

00:18:34.241 --> 00:18:39.241
There's a town in Italy, or
it might have been France,

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:41.683
this amazing wine-producing,

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:45.210
their family, world famous for their wine,

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:46.242
and the Nazi's were coming through,

00:18:46.242 --> 00:18:47.945
and they didn't want the Nazi's to get it,

00:18:47.945 --> 00:18:51.210
so they hid the wine in a cave,

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:53.979
and bricked it over,
and then put moss on it,

00:18:53.979 --> 00:18:56.970
and made it look aged, and
then the Nazi's came in

00:18:56.970 --> 00:18:59.118
looking for the wine, and
they couldn't find it.

00:18:59.118 --> 00:19:02.496
And they scoured the countryside,
and they decided that

00:19:02.496 --> 00:19:04.438
whoever's the next person
that comes out on the street,

00:19:04.438 --> 00:19:05.271
they're gonna torture them

00:19:05.271 --> 00:19:07.017
and find out where the wine is hidden.

00:19:07.017 --> 00:19:10.850
So the townspeople agreed
to let the prisoner,

00:19:11.787 --> 00:19:14.440
out of the, they said,
"you're free to go."

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:16.459
And because the prisoner
didn't know any of this,

00:19:16.459 --> 00:19:17.718
the prisoner was just a thing.

00:19:17.718 --> 00:19:20.588
The prisoner comes out,
the Nazi's torture him,

00:19:20.588 --> 00:19:22.516
and they couldn't figure out where it was

00:19:22.516 --> 00:19:24.244
and the Nazi's leave.

00:19:24.244 --> 00:19:25.692
- It would have been hilarious
of the guy they tortured was

00:19:25.692 --> 00:19:28.310
a Sombiere and they just killed court.

00:19:28.310 --> 00:19:29.143
Come on man.

00:19:29.143 --> 00:19:31.497
I just go my degree, really?

00:19:31.497 --> 00:19:33.370
- What are you doing in jail though?

