to have deep personal
relationships, and things like that,

00:43:05.053 --> 00:43:07.027
and so I think that whatever we do with

00:43:07.027 --> 00:43:09.562
genetic modification, we
shouldn't interfere with those

00:43:09.562 --> 00:43:11.395
core human capacities,

00:43:14.459 --> 00:43:16.202
and the flip side of that is

00:43:16.202 --> 00:43:19.289
if an embryo, like an
offspring doesn't have

00:43:19.289 --> 00:43:21.296
those capacities, then
we should try to make

00:43:21.296 --> 00:43:22.444
sure that they those--

00:43:22.444 --> 00:43:24.980
- [Neil] In whatever
genetic way, if possible.

00:43:24.980 --> 00:43:27.728
And beyond that it's just luxury items

00:43:27.728 --> 00:43:30.186
off of a shopping list (laughing).

00:43:30.186 --> 00:43:31.019
(ding)

00:43:31.019 --> 00:43:32.231
All right, next.

00:43:32.231 --> 00:43:34.210
We've got time for one more.

00:43:34.210 --> 00:43:35.100
- Here we go.

00:43:35.100 --> 00:43:37.034
- Better be a good one, dude.

00:43:37.034 --> 00:43:39.249
- Wow, there's a lot of pressure here.

00:43:39.249 --> 00:43:42.978
Okay, this is Dagan Pleak, Instagram,

00:43:42.978 --> 00:43:45.942
"Will we attempt to splice human DNA with

00:43:45.942 --> 00:43:49.226
"other animal DNA to
make mutants of a sort?

00:43:49.226 --> 00:43:50.748
"Would this conflict with our ethics

00:43:50.748 --> 00:43:52.854
"and what are you thoughts on creating

00:43:52.854 --> 00:43:54.279
"new humanoid species?"

00:43:54.279 --> 00:43:55.512
It's called the centaur isn't it?

00:43:55.512 --> 00:43:56.667
(group laughing)

00:43:56.667 --> 00:43:58.000
- Or a minotaur.

00:43:59.379 --> 00:44:00.514
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:44:00.514 --> 00:44:02.462
So it relates to what I just said earlier.

00:44:02.462 --> 00:44:04.964
I think as long as we don't affect those

00:44:04.964 --> 00:44:08.870
core fundamental capacities,
sometimes we might look into

00:44:08.870 --> 00:44:13.468
these type of augments, these
combining different genes.

00:44:13.468 --> 00:44:16.047
- What animal would you
want to splice with a human?

00:44:16.047 --> 00:44:16.880
- I can tell you.

00:44:16.880 --> 00:44:17.752
- Would you wanna be?

00:44:17.752 --> 00:44:18.657
Let me guess.

00:44:18.657 --> 00:44:20.762
A dog, so you could sniff.

00:44:20.762 --> 00:44:23.792
- In my concluding
remarks, I will tell you.

00:44:23.792 --> 00:44:24.625
Yes.

00:44:25.654 --> 00:44:28.917
So we got time for just
some reflective thoughts.

00:44:28.917 --> 00:44:31.140
So Paul why don't you go first.

00:44:31.140 --> 00:44:33.094
- I just think that all of these questions

00:44:33.094 --> 00:44:35.140
that you deal with, it's
endlessly fascinating

00:44:35.140 --> 00:44:37.312
and on some level open-ended.

00:44:37.312 --> 00:44:40.973
You seem to have the most
subjective job in a way.

00:44:40.973 --> 00:44:43.920
- Plus you're like the
calmest person I've ever met.

00:44:43.920 --> 00:44:45.427
- Which means you're up to no good.

00:44:45.427 --> 00:44:46.434
- He's hiding something.

00:44:46.434 --> 00:44:49.912
- Because you know
something that we don't.

00:44:49.912 --> 00:44:52.550
And, we didn't get much into this,

00:44:52.550 --> 00:44:55.145
but I know you've done a lot of work with

00:44:55.145 --> 00:44:59.502
manipulation of memory for
PTSD, rape victims, etc.,

00:44:59.502 --> 00:45:00.714
and erasing thought.

00:45:00.714 --> 00:45:02.540
Is that making advancements--

00:45:02.540 --> 00:45:04.054
- [Neil] Was that part of your TED Talk?

00:45:04.054 --> 00:45:04.919
- Yes.

00:45:04.919 --> 00:45:06.629
- And can I have it in September because

00:45:06.629 --> 00:45:08.565
I'm going to a reunion in high school

00:45:08.565 --> 00:45:11.093
and I wanna wipe out the memory of

00:45:11.093 --> 00:45:13.805
asking Renee Sherlock to the prom

00:45:13.805 --> 00:45:15.614
and getting turned down twice.

00:45:15.614 --> 00:45:17.116
I wanna wipe out her memory and mine.

00:45:17.116 --> 00:45:17.958
- And both memories.

00:45:17.958 --> 00:45:18.988
Oh yeah.

00:45:18.988 --> 00:45:21.241
- Take some propranolol with you.

00:45:21.241 --> 00:45:22.405
- I knew you were a drug dealer.

00:45:22.405 --> 00:45:23.238
- He's got it.

00:45:23.238 --> 00:45:24.131
He's got the drugs.

00:45:24.131 --> 00:45:25.988
- Is that fairly far along?

00:45:25.988 --> 00:45:28.873
- That's pretty far along
but unfortunately you gotta

00:45:28.873 --> 00:45:32.210
take it within 12 hours of
asking someone to a prom so.

00:45:32.210 --> 00:45:33.395
(group laughing)

00:45:33.395 --> 00:45:34.963
- Oh, so it erases your short-term memory.

00:45:34.963 --> 00:45:36.048
- [Matthew] That's right.

00:45:36.048 --> 00:45:40.195
It stops it from consolidating
into the long-term memory.

00:45:40.195 --> 00:45:43.862
There's another thing,
something called Zip.

00:45:45.029 --> 00:45:46.720
So there's this idea that--

00:45:46.720 --> 00:45:48.844
- I'm not consuming anything called Zip.

00:45:48.844 --> 00:45:50.223
(group laughing)

00:45:50.223 --> 00:45:51.523
- Zip erases everything.

00:45:51.523 --> 00:45:52.364
- Really?

00:45:52.364 --> 00:45:53.197
- Yeah.

00:45:53.197 --> 00:45:55.755
- Wow, I'll see you after the show.

00:45:55.755 --> 00:45:57.656
- So, Matthew, give us some

00:45:57.656 --> 00:46:00.022
reflective concluding remarks here.

00:46:00.022 --> 00:46:04.259
- So, I think there are a
lot of these new technologies

00:46:04.259 --> 00:46:06.166
that are on the horizon,

00:46:06.166 --> 00:46:08.113
I think they have a lot of promises,

00:46:08.113 --> 00:46:11.517
but we also should also
be mindful of their

00:46:11.517 --> 00:46:14.684
ethical implications, and I they can--

00:46:15.977 --> 00:46:17.293
- Further keeping you employed.

00:46:17.293 --> 00:46:18.214
- That's right.

00:46:18.214 --> 00:46:19.593
So that it keeps me employed.

00:46:19.593 --> 00:46:20.847
- That's hilarious.

00:46:20.847 --> 00:46:23.121
He keeps raising issues
that aren't issues.

00:46:23.121 --> 00:46:24.306
No that's an issue, it is--

00:46:24.306 --> 00:46:25.644
- It's not an issue yet, but it will be.

00:46:25.644 --> 00:46:26.477
- It's an issue.

00:46:26.477 --> 00:46:29.461
My kids going to college
next year, it's an issue.

00:46:29.461 --> 00:46:33.044
- Yeah and I think
ultimately our aim is to

00:46:35.612 --> 00:46:37.887
create human well-being,
human flourishing,

00:46:37.887 --> 00:46:42.287
and so we wanna make sure that
these technologies do that.

00:46:42.287 --> 00:46:45.882
- So here's what I think,
not that anyone asked,

00:46:45.882 --> 00:46:47.977
- Wait, Neil what do you think?

00:46:47.977 --> 00:46:49.977
- [Neil] Thank you Paul!

00:46:50.844 --> 00:46:54.677
The fact that you can
cross-breed the genetics

00:46:56.135 --> 00:46:59.949
of different species at
all, we do this often

00:46:59.949 --> 00:47:04.195
in the food chain, is a
reminder that all life

00:47:04.195 --> 00:47:05.862
has some common DNA.

00:47:06.819 --> 00:47:09.236
So we should not be surprised that you can

00:47:09.236 --> 00:47:12.293
take a fish DNA and put it in a tomato.

00:47:12.293 --> 00:47:16.206
Just a reminder that we're
all related, genetically.

00:47:16.206 --> 00:47:18.027
So what I think to myself is,

00:47:18.027 --> 00:47:22.277
the human form is not some
perfect example of life,

00:47:24.950 --> 00:47:29.255
I like the fact that newts
can regenerate their limbs.

00:47:29.255 --> 00:47:31.581
Where is the gene sequence for that?

00:47:31.581 --> 00:47:34.861
Let's give that to humans
and give it first to veterans

00:47:34.861 --> 00:47:39.570
who have lost their legs or
arms and regrow our limbs.

00:47:39.570 --> 00:47:42.310
If a newt can do it and
we have genetic editing,

00:47:42.310 --> 00:47:43.532
why can't we do it?

00:47:43.532 --> 00:47:44.460
- And why haven't we?

00:47:44.460 --> 00:47:47.539
- [Neil] Well, maybe that's
to come, but look at what is

00:47:47.539 --> 00:47:51.563
possible in the experimentation
of the biodiversity

00:47:51.563 --> 00:47:53.892
that is life on earth and say,

00:47:53.892 --> 00:47:56.824
why can't we have some of that?

00:47:56.824 --> 00:48:00.441
And that is a thought from
the cosmic perspective.

00:48:00.441 --> 00:48:02.594
I wanna thank Matthew Liao.

00:48:02.594 --> 00:48:05.350
Second time on StarTalk, we
will bring you back for sure.

00:48:05.350 --> 00:48:06.232
- Thank you.

00:48:06.232 --> 00:48:08.982
- [Neil] All right and work hard,

00:48:10.192 --> 00:48:11.466
make a better world for us,

00:48:11.466 --> 00:48:14.491
or help us make a better
world for ourselves.

00:48:14.491 --> 00:48:16.970
- Keep creating issues
that aren't really issues

00:48:16.970 --> 00:48:18.358
so you have a job.

00:48:18.358 --> 00:48:21.358
By the way, best mind eraser, vodka.

00:48:22.631 --> 00:48:24.846
It's already been invented.

00:48:24.846 --> 00:48:26.663
- [Neil] Takes out
those cells right there.

00:48:26.663 --> 00:48:27.621
Paul, always good to have you.

00:48:27.621 --> 00:48:28.569
- Thank you, it's a lot of fun.

00:48:28.569 --> 00:48:31.038
- [Neil] All right, I've
been and will continue to be

00:48:31.038 --> 00:48:34.340
Neil deGrasse Tyson, your
personal astrophysicist,

00:48:34.340 --> 00:48:35.830
coming to you from my office,

00:48:35.830 --> 00:48:37.103
at the Hayden Planetarium

00:48:37.103 --> 00:48:39.434
at the American Museum of Natural History,

00:48:39.434 --> 00:48:42.234
and as always I bid
you to keep looking up.

00:48:42.234 --> 00:48:44.817
(upbeat music)

